Title:
Vipere — VS Installer Elevation Service LPE + Persistence via AppDomainManager Hijacking (ETW-off)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Vipere is a Cobalt Strike/Adaptix BOF that abuses a chain of weaknesses in the Visual Studio Installer Elevation Service to gain persistent SYSTEM code execution that can be re-triggered by any authenticated user. The chain relies on permissive service SDDL (allowing Authenticated Users to start the service), lack of integrity/signature enforcement for .NET `.config`-driven CLR behaviors, and an orphaned service registration that can persist after VS uninstall. It achieves execution by injecting a merged `VSInstallerElevationService.exe.config` that disables ETW, bypasses strong-name trust checks, and loads a custom AppDomainManager which `LoadLibrary`s a beacon DLL in-process. The AppDomainManager also handles service control flow (`StartServiceCtrlDispatcherW` / `SERVICE_RUNNING`) to keep the service alive without spawning child processes, reducing EDR parent/child telemetry. A secondary persistence mode copies a legitimate .NET VS binary (`vs_installershell.exe`) to ProgramData and runs it as SYSTEM via a scheduled task at logon with the same AppDomainManager hijack. This is primarily useful for red teams and operators looking for stealthy SYSTEM execution and durable persistence using signed Microsoft binaries and configuration-based CLR abuse.

Technical Focus:
- Visual Studio Installer Elevation Service abuse (service SDDL/start permissions)
- .NET AppDomainManager hijacking via application configuration
- ETW suppression via `.config` (`<etwEnable enabled="false"/>`)
- Strong-name / trusted app strong name bypass via CLR config
- In-process DLL loading (LoadLibrary) inside a signed service binary
- Persistence via Scheduled Tasks + orphaned SCM registration

Use Cases:
- Achieve SYSTEM execution from an operator-controlled DLL without replacing signed binaries
- Establish persistence that survives reboot/logon and even VS uninstallation artifacts
- Re-trigger SYSTEM beaconing by allowing any authenticated user to start the service (`sc start ...`)
- Tradecraft research on CLR config abuse, ETW visibility gaps, and service lifecycle spoofing
- Adversary emulation of AppDomainManager-based in-the-wild techniques

Keywords:
